      "text": "To solve for $t$, we consider that $11$ and $10$ are relatively prime, so we will work with powers of $11$ and find a cycle that matches the condition. First, we calculate the last two digits of $11$ raised to powers until we find a match for $6$. When $i=t$, the value will be $6$. We observe the sequence of last two digits:\n$(11^1=11, 11^2=121, 11^3=1331, 11^4=1441, ...)$\nWe notice the pattern: the last two digits are determined by $11^2$ and $11^4$, which are \"21\". By dividing and subtracting from 36, we find:\n$36-21=17$\nThe last two digits will match with $11$ multiplied by 2. Since we have found a cycle length of $8$:\n$(10\\cdot 2=20, 10\\cdot 4=40, 2 \\mod 18=20, 4 \\mod 18=40)$\nThus, we can conclude that $i=2$, and $11\\cdot 2=32, 11\\cdot 4=44, 36-44=-8, 6-18=-12$.\nSo, $t=2$. The answer is $boxed{2}$.",
