      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the modular inverse of 11 modulo 100. The modular inverse of a number \\( a \\) modulo \\( n \\) is a number \\( b \\) such that:\n\n\\[\nab \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}\n\\]\n\nWe will use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start with the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 11 and 100:\n\n1. \\( 100 = 11 \\cdot 9 + 1 \\)\n2. \\( 11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nThe gcd is 1, confirming that 11 and 100 are coprime, and thus an inverse exists. Now we backtrack to express 1 as a combination of 11 and 100:\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 11 \\cdot 9\n\\]\n\nRewriting 11 as 11, we get:\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 99\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 11 \\cdot 9\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( b = -9 \\) is a solution to \\( 11b \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100} \\). Since we need a positive inverse, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\n\nSo, the modular inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can solve the original congruence using this inverse:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\n\nNext, reduce 3276 modulo 100:\n\\[\n3276 \\div 100 = 32 \\text{ remainder } 76\n\\]\n\\[\n3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\( t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\). Since we need the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\), we have:\n\\[\nt = 76\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
